I verified end-to-end reachability first: pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10) can currently ping each other bidirectionally with 0% loss and ~1.4–1.6 ms RTT, so the reported outage is not present now. Core control-plane health along the path is also good. On the OSPF side, r1 and r11 are Full neighbors on segment B (r1 eth1 111.1.1.1 ↔ r11 eth1 111.1.1.2), with stable uptimes. On the EIGRP side, r11 and r12 are exchanging routes on segment EE (r11 eth0 112.1.1.1 ↔ r12 eth0 112.1.1.2); r11’s routing table contains EIGRP-learned networks via 112.1.1.2 and r12 advertises 120.0.0.0/8 directly on eth1, indicating a working handoff to the 120/8 LAN where pc12 resides. All involved interfaces (r1 eth4 to 20/8, r1 eth1 to 111/8, r11 eth0 to 112/8, r12 eth1 to 120/8) are up with carrier and no errors. Mechanistically, if an interior backbone link were down, we would see OSPF neighbor loss on r1–r11 or missing EIGRP routes on r11, and pings would fail; none of these symptoms are present. Root cause: not reproducible now—likely a transient during the maintenance window that has since been restored. Corrective fix: no action required. Recommend closing the ticket and reviewing maintenance change logs/FRR adjacency logs for transient drops during the window, and enable link/adjacency monitoring to alert on future events. 
